      service links sit under the text rather than in the frozen trailing slot,
      which adds 12 + 34 below the 48. See item_player_history_track.xml for the
      measurement behind that - beside the text they left the title column 113 at
      the frozen width and real names wrapped hard. 120 is what the row comes to,
      not a number set anywhere, so it has no dimen; this one stays because the
      section's empty and loading states reserve exactly the frozen band.
    -->
    <dimen name="player_history_row_height">74dp</dimen>
    <!--
      13 across, 17 down, and the split is what keeps the frozen row at 74 while
      the text block inside it is 40 rather than the mock's 48.

      The frozen mock spends its 74 as 13 + 48 + 13 and hides the cover being 8
      shorter than the text by centring it, which put the cover at y=17 and the
      timestamp at y=27. With the block at 40 those two would have risen to 13 and
      23 and the row shrunk to 66. 17 down puts both back exactly where the mock
      has them - cover 17, timestamp 27 - and the row back at 17 + 40 + 17 = 74, so
      three rows and the button still make the frozen 374.

      Horizontal stays 13: the row's across-the-page spec is 13 + 42 + 12 + 40 + 12
      + text + 13 and none of it moves. Hence two dimens and four explicit padding
      attributes on the row rather than one android:padding.
    -->
    <dimen name="player_history_row_padding">13dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_row_padding_vertical">17dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_row_gap">12dp</dimen>
    <!--
      42, not the frozen 38.98, because the frozen box cannot hold a real
      timestamp. Onest has no tabular figures - its GSUB carries only calt, ccmp
      and locl, so fontFeatureSettings="tnum" is a no-op here - and its digits
      are proportional to a degree that matters: '1' advances 363 units where
      '0' advances 665, nearly double. At 14sp that makes "08:11" 31.72dp and
      "00:00" 40.78dp, so the widest HH:MM overflowed 38.98 and lost its last
      digit while narrower ones fitted.

      42 rather than 41, because the advances are not the whole width: Paint
      measures "00:00" at 41.38dp once this font's kerning is applied, so a box
      sized to the 40.78 the advances add up to still clipped. The number to
      trust is the one PlayerHistoryLayoutTest takes from the view's own paint.

      It stays a fixed width rather than becoming wrap_content with a minWidth,
      which is how every other frozen number in this row was relaxed. The
      artwork is constrained to the end of this view, so a self-sizing time
      column would start each row's artwork at a different x - trading a clipped
      digit for a ragged artwork edge down the section.

      The text inside it is START aligned, by owner correction, so every
      timestamp begins on the same vertical line. That is the whole reason the
      box is fixed rather than hugging: a fixed box holds both alignments at
      once, the timestamps' left edge and the artwork's.
    -->
    <dimen name="player_history_time_width">42dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_time_height">20dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_artwork_size">40dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_artwork_corner">6dp</dimen>
    <!--
      History row line heights, local to this surface.

      The shared tokens set the title on a 28 line and the artist on a 20, which
      is the FINAL Figma leading, and 28 + 20 = 48 - a text block 8 taller than
      the 40 cover beside it. Owner intent is that the normal one-line title over
      one-line artist reads inside the cover, so the leading comes off here and
      only here.

      These are the font's own natural line, not invented numbers. Measured on
      device through the view's paint with includeFontPadding=false, Onest Regular
      gives descent-ascent of 57px at 17sp and 47px at 14sp, which at 2.625 is
      21.71dp and 17.90dp. 22 and 18 are the next whole values above those, so
      nothing clips - the slack is 0.29dp and 0.10dp - and they sum to exactly 40.

      Applied in PlayerHistoryAdapter, not here: values/typography.xml is
      generated from the Figma export and says not to hand-edit, so a new token
      does not belong in it, and MyataTypography.Factory would overwrite a line
      height set on the view in XML anyway. The adapter sets it after inflation,
      which is the one place that wins.
    -->
    <dimen name="player_history_title_line_height">22sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_artist_line_height">18sp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_title_height">22dp</dimen>
    <dimen name="player_history_artist_height">18dp</dimen>

